      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The high-pitched whining sound is typically a normal characteristic of a turbocharged engine and is sometimes referred to as \"turbine whine.\" This sound comes from the phenomenon known as \"turbo whine,\" which occurs due to the rapid spinning of the turbine shaft. As the turbine spins, it imparts a vibration to the exhaust manifold and into the manifold pipe, manifesting as a noticeable whistling or whirring sound. Most people hear this during transitions where the turbocharger ramps up to boost pressure—either during throttle body's increase or overdrive conditions. It's important to know, however, that this noise should not be excessively loud or accompanied by a significant loss in engine performance or power. If you're reporting this as an issue during your exam, list it as a normal operational characteristic and distinguish it from symptoms exhibiting mechanical issues, like turbo lag or inefficient turbine performance.",
